## Why This Is Asked

Assess familiarity with Linux process scheduling, cgroups, systemd properties, and practical experimentation. The scenario emphasizes beginner-friendly diagnostic steps and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Linux CPU scheduling basics (CFS, CPU shares vs quotas)
- Systemd resource controls (CPUQuota, CPUQuotaPeriodUSec, cpuset)
- Tools: mpstat, pidstat, iostat, systemd-cgls

## Code Example

```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const services = ['critical-api', 'worker-a'];
services.forEach(s=>{
  try{
    const quota = execSync(`systemctl show ${s} -p CPUQuota --value`, {encoding:'utf8'}).trim();
    const per = execSync(`systemctl show ${s} -p CPUQuotaPeriodUSec --value`, {encoding:'utf8'}).trim();
    console.log(`${s}: CPUQuota=${quota||'0'}, PeriodUSec=${per||'0'}`);
  }catch(e){
    console.error(`err ${s}:`, e.message);
  }
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Which steps would you automate to monitor for burst-induced latency and what signals would you alert on?
- What trade-offs exist between CPUQuota vs cpuset in terms of scheduling fairness and contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines ability to tame CPU scheduling and memory reclaim in multi-tenant Linux deployments under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 cpu.max and CPU weight for per-tenant QoS
- cpuset and CPU affinity to confine tenants
- memory reclaim behavior and swappiness tuning
- BPF-based observability for per-tenant latency, IRQs, cache misses
- phased testing, safe rollback, and controlled rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight latency probe template (pseudo)
async function measureLatency(endpoint){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(endpoint);
  return performance.now() - t0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify safe rollback criteria during staged tests?
- Which metrics dashboards and alerts would you configure for sustained SLOs?
